&lt;h2&gt;


  What Is Raksha Bandhan?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raksha Bandhan, commonly known as Rakhi, is a Hindu festival celebrated on the full moon (Purnima) of the month of Shravana. On this day, sisters tie a sacred thread around their brothers' wrists as a symbol of love and prayers for their happiness and prosperity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In return, brothers express their gratitude by promising lifelong support and protection while exchanging gifts and blessings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, Raksha Bandhan is also celebrated among cousins, close friends, and even those who share a bond of mutual care and respect.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Is Raksha Bandhan Celebrated?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The festival reminds us that relationships are strengthened through love, trust, responsibility, and compassion.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raksha Bandhan celebrates:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Family values&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mutual respect&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Emotional connection&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lifelong support&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cultural traditions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gratitude and affection&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It continues to bring families together across generations.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Traditional Raksha Bandhan Rituals
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most families celebrate the festival by following these traditions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decorating a Rakhi thali&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Performing aarti&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Applying tilak&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tying the Rakhi&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Offering sweets&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Exchanging gifts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Seeking blessings from elders&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Spending quality time with family&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These customs make the occasion meaningful and memorable.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Cultural Importance of Raksha Bandhan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raksha Bandhan reflects India's rich cultural heritage and emphasizes the importance of family relationships.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Beyond biological siblings, many people tie Rakhi to cousins, mentors, friends, and individuals they deeply respect, highlighting the festival's universal message of love and care.&lt;/p&gt;




